{"title":"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>This is a timed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minute, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> question exam. Only pencils, pens, scratch paper, and erasers are allowed.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A gardener stands in a triangular courtyard <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> with side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/a/1faf03033f1dba03fb05985e5ef5eb60dcdff425.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13, 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> meters. He places a pivot point on the southern boundary line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> which has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820a20de2847dfdc1bc5cffc8d904933150032bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> meters and spins a long, horizontal pruning stick around it, which clears a circular path <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />. If the stick is exactly long enough to just graze the two adjacent walls <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> as it spins, what is the radius of the area swept by the stick in meters?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/9/d7933d9ddb1dc62b0256735f36927fa43ccaefb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{8}{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } \\frac{10}{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 4 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } \\frac{17}{4} \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"385\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the sum of the last three digits of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/f/17fc62f45f903ea5a7361853a16f8dce85bf45c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2024 + 2025)^{2026}\\cdot 2027.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"193\" height=\"21\" /> \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/8/988db38dec881d8b15bb0da24795397f51b58dd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 1 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) }13 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 11 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 7 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"373\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find, rounded to the nearest multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the sum of all fractions in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f159909a995f3eead00a6fa1f41b5bd1b8264a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2024 + 24x}{2025 + 25x},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"98\" height=\"40\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is some nonnegative integer, such that when fully simplified, the denominator is exactly one more than the numerator.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31b8226f323c3087826e7ea0f17e824b2f67ae33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 8 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) }12 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 16 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 20 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"392\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 3\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b56d32444d8efd65eb0cb6c01f8ac72c32587e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are real numbers that satisfy the system of equations below.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9863eb481b1bbe61e71bb1e23723c850c6e5dbd0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{cases} \\tan(x) \\cdot \\tan(y) &amp;= \\frac{1}{3}\\\\ \\tan(x) \\cdot \\tan(z) &amp;= \\frac{1}{5}\\\\ \\tan(y) \\cdot \\tan(z) &amp;= \\frac{1}{7} \\end{cases}\\]\" width=\"171\" height=\"74\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/8/b28bf6c0e1552aa67bf0993942598b5e6e2359cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos(2x + 2y + 2z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"141\" height=\"20\" /> can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/8/888fed89e19182447f85b3cb6676548a49395284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"27\" height=\"34\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime integers. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/1/c91f584215242319c296604fac17fd4c0567c508.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{m + n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> rounded to the nearest hundredth?\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/d/a7dc377a91146d081efc1f4ba0c4cd157fe96232.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 16.00 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 17.00 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 18.00 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 22.63 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 25.46$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"517\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 4\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Nathan is playing a game where he has to guess whether a card is blue or red. He starts off with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards, and he knows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of them are blue and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of them are red. Nathan always plays optimally. An example scenario of the game for the first three turns is shown below. The total expected number of cards he will correctly guess by the end of the game can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/b/53bf1afcb274a3bb84338109fffec8a1f1c02008.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><b>Example scenario for first three turns:</b>\n</p>\n<ul><li>Nathan guesses blue on the first turn</li>\n<li>His first turn guess turns out to be correct</li>\n<li>Nathan guesses blue on the second turn</li>\n<li>His second turn guess turns out to be correct</li>\n<li>Nathan will choose to guess red on the third turn</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7474e70a5eea8a4c5730f9317c1b26ac0677b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 204 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 239 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 169 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 134 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 180$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018110301\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.020 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 173/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6677/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36055:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018110301 and revision id 282000. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}